A demo whose output is a scalar or otherwise tiny grid establishes only that observed input-output mapping; it does not show that the answer is independent of the input. Never copy a 1x1 output into the test submission solely because it was the demo output. Relate the scalar to the demo's distinguishing structure, then check that candidate feature against the test grid.

After an incorrect compact-output submission, freeze the failed answer and revisit both (a) why the task compresses or selects the input and (b) which source structure determines the output. Binary feedback does not reveal whether shape, coordinates, values, or selection was wrong, so do not make a cosmetic variation or request demos reflexively. Name at least two plausible rules, their differing test predictions, and a concrete feature a new demo could discriminate before paying for one. If the distinction is not test-relevant, use the strongest structurally supported hypothesis and validate dimensions and serialization.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545241/attempts/ae362c74672c4cdab9f17b35f9b74337/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-solving/references/tiny-demo-output-and-recovery.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/global-pattern-check.md", "content": "# Global-pattern checks in demo-driven ARC

Before settling on a local object interpretation, test whether the complete grid is organized by global periodicity, reflection, or wraparound. Compare aligned rows, columns, and diagonals, including blank regions, to identify whether apparent gaps are part of a repeated sequence. Treat these only as hypotheses: reconstruct the entire demo output cell by cell before using a pattern on the test input. A visually plausible completion is not sufficient evidence; if multiple completions fit, request a demo that distinguishes them or avoid an unsupported guess.
